<</nobr>>

:: Day5_SQ_PhotoReview [day5 sidequest home lily photoreview]
<<nobr>>
<<centerImage "image/events/lily_bedroom.png">>

<p>I pass Lily's room on the way to the bathroom. Her door is cracked. Not an invitation. Lily doesn't do invitations. She does traps that look like open doors.</p>
<p>Through the crack I can see her at her desk. Laptop open. Phone propped beside it. She's scrolling through photos on her laptop, and even at this angle, even through a two-inch gap, I can see that some of those photos are of me.</p>
<p>She's editing. She's sitting at her desk with photo editing software open and she's touching up images of my painted toenails, adjusting the color saturation, warming the tones, cropping tight so the images look anonymous and aesthetic and ready for the feed she's building.</p>
<p>A grid of photos on her screen. The toenails from multiple angles. The waistband shot, cropped to show only the lace and the bow, no face, no identifying features. A photo I didn't know she had: my hand wrapped around a coffee cup, the faint clear gloss on my nails catching the light. When did she take that? How many photos exist that I don't know about?</p>
<p>She's humming. Working. Happy. Building Lexi pixel by pixel from stolen moments and careful angles, constructing a person who looks feminine and mysterious and just discovering herself, a person who has my body and my nails and my waistband but not my face. Not yet.</p>
<p>The corkboard above her desk has been updated since yesterday. The @prettyboykai printout is still there. The PROJECT L sheet is still there. But new additions: engagement statistics. Comments from the twelve (now eighteen) followers. Screenshotted DMs from people asking "is this you?" and "more please" and "do you sell content?"</p>
<p>Sell content. She's looking at monetization. She's building an audience for a person who doesn't know they're a product yet.</p>
<p>I back away from the door. My stomach is tight. My cock, traitorously, is not. There's something about being seen that terrifies the civilized parts of me and excites the parts that have been growing in the dark like mushrooms in a cellar. The parts that wear panties to bed and call women "ma'am" and let strangers moisturize my face and haven't fought any of it. Not really. Not hard enough.</p>
<p>Lexi is growing. And the thing that scares me most isn't that she exists. It's that I want to see what she looks like when she's finished.</p>

<<set $lilyPhotoEditing to true>>
<<set $lexiFollowers to 18>>
<<set $day5_lilyPhotoReview to true>>
<<spendTime 1>>
<<set $currentMapLocation to "home">>

